<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Revolutionary Government of the Philippines</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1899 CE · capital Bacoor (later Malolos)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Government set up by Emilio Aguinaldo in 1898 that declared Philippine independence from Spain and prepared the Malolos Republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> destroyed the Spanish fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> Bay on 1 May 1898, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and on 24 May proclaimed a dictatorial government at Cavite. He declared independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawit">Kawit</a> on 12 June 1898, and on the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apolinario-mabini">Apolinario Mabini</a> converted his regime into a Revolutionary Government on 23 June.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Aguinaldo</a> ruled as president with a cabinet of secretaries, while provincial and municipal governments were organised in liberated areas. Revolutionary armies took most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> from Spanish garrisons. A congress met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barasoain-church">Barasoain church</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malolos">Malolos</a> in September 1898 and drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malolos-constitution">Malolos Constitution</a>, drawing on Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American models.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> refused to recognise the government, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a> of December 1898 transferred the islands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to America. The Revolutionary Government gave way to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-philippine-republic">First Philippine Republic</a> on 23 January 1899.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines.jpg" alt="Revolutionary Government of the Philippines" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines_(1898%E2%80%931901).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1899 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bacoor (later Malolos)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emilio Aguinaldo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tagalog, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dictatorial Government of the Philippines</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines" aria-label="Revolutionary Government of the Philippines on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7989 1639 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Revolutionary Government of the Philippines lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8309 1895L8309 1890M8309 1895L8302 1881"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8309 1890h0M8302 1881h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8309 1895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8309 1895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bacoor. <a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
